    },
    {
      "id": "N_IA2bPcteWUFf7Hr5QxJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1318.6142159127426,
      "y": 106001.83973847446,
      "width": 572,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bw9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 825527229,
      "version": 63,
      "versionNonce": 1684933181,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781324714571,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Workflow is of two types -> Sequential, Event Driven",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Workflow is of two types -> Sequential, Event Driven",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "VqnbH8IzV_gO2I3C8xQQJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1323.3798409127426,
      "y": 106056.83973847446,
      "width": 1034,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2084099773,
      "version": 252,
      "versionNonce": 831886291,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781324727306,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Sequential\nprompt -> LLM -> promp -> LLM -> result -> this flow is executed sequentially without stopping\n\nEvent Driven ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Sequential\nprompt -> LLM -> promp -> LLM -> result -> this flow is executed sequentially without stopping\n\nEvent Driven ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "C2nDlIHtsom-wl_k7ec6G",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1378.0634346627426,
      "y": 106166.56630097446,
      "width": 1111,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 431378643,
      "version": 1604,
      "versionNonce": 183835805,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781325355340,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "lets say we have create one workflow, and that is pause in between, and that is waiting for\nsome external trigger, when this trigger will come, then workflow will receive this and move\nforward. This is called event driven execution.\n\nLIke after postJD we will monitor after 7 days only, we have to pause the workflow 7 days\nwhen we get this trigger 7 days completed then flow will be moved.\nex 2 -> after sending offer letter we need to wait for accept or reject by User.\n\nSo when we create a complex Agentic AI workflow, most of the time we will perform event\ndriven execution.\n\nLangChain is not built for Event Driven Execution, Langchain was built for Sequential execution.\nLangchain is for chain starts then it will complete,it is not going to stop in between. this \nfunctionality is not inside LangChain.\n\nSO to make our Logic in LangChain we need to create multiple chains and we need to manage all\nand for this we need to write more glue code for even handing and state transfer.\n\n\nLangGraph provide us Event drive execution option. our whole execution is stateful here,we can\nstore state in our memory or database at any node, and afer that lets say we are waiting for external\ntrigger, once this comes we will go and see what was current state and resume accordingly.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "lets say we have create one workflow, and that is pause in between, and that is waiting for\nsome external trigger, when this trigger will come, then workflow will receive this and move\nforward. This is called event driven execution.\n\nLIke after postJD we will monitor after 7 days only, we have to pause the workflow 7 days\nwhen we get this trigger 7 days completed then flow will be moved.\nex 2 -> after sending offer letter we need to wait for accept or reject by User.\n\nSo when we create a complex Agentic AI workflow, most of the time we will perform event\ndriven execution.\n\nLangChain is not built for Event Driven Execution, Langchain was built for Sequential execution.\nLangchain is for chain starts then it will complete,it is not going to stop in between. this \nfunctionality is not inside LangChain.\n\nSO to make our Logic in LangChain we need to create multiple chains and we need to manage all\nand for this we need to write more glue code for even handing and state transfer.\n\n\nLangGraph provide us Event drive execution option. our whole execution is stateful here,we can\nstore state in our memory or database at any node, and afer that lets say we are waiting for external\ntrigger, once this comes we will go and see what was current state and resume accordingly.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5J8mlJ7WJfhjqx9ezMYyv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1261.3798409127426,
      "y": 106784.83973847446,
      "width": 330,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 214508989,
      "version": 43,
      "versionNonce": 107906099,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781325385322,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Challenge 4 -> Fault Tolerance",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Challenge 4 -> Fault Tolerance",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RlfVVrgpG2DpLjJPIo5zM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1318.5243721627426,
      "y": 106820.92176972446,
      "width": 1078,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwH",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1198943507,
      "version": 2107,
      "versionNonce": 1903409053,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781326326859,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "meaning of Fault tolerance is if any issue is there in system, system can handle it by itself.\nFault tolerance is very important in long running workflows\n\nOur workflow is long workflow it take many days to complete. this type of workflow has high \nrisk of fault(small like in postjd linkedIN API is not working)(big falut like we have deployed\nour workflow in AwS and that AWS server itself is down). so ideally our system should be \ncapable enough to handle these 2 fautls.\n\nIf we will create whole workflow in LangChain, LangChain doesn't have Fault tolerance. LIke \nlets say we have create a Chain that has 5 steps and our system is down lets say at 3rd step,\nin that case we need to execute this chain from start. Because LangChain doesn't have any\nconcept of fault tolerance. Fault tolerance means from where our system break from there only\nit will start. LangChains assume it chains are short lived. due to that they think fault tolerance\nis not much important here.\n\nIn LangGraph we have builtin fault tolerance, LangGraph gives fault tolerance in both faults\nsmall or big, for small problems it gives concept of retrying lets say at postjd LinkedIN api\nis not working so we can write our code in try catch if any issue retry. with the help of retry\nlogic we can handle small level faults.\n\nfor big faults, system level faults, our server is down, our docker container is down, in this \nsituation LangGraph has concept of recovery, in this letsay  at 3rd node server is down, in this\nsituation we can resume exaction from here only, and next node will execute it will not happen\nthat from first it will restart again. we get both retry and recovery in built in LangGraph.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "meaning of Fault tolerance is if any issue is there in system, system can handle it by itself.\nFault tolerance is very important in long running workflows\n\nOur workflow is long workflow it take many days to complete. this type of workflow has high \nrisk of fault(small like in postjd linkedIN API is not working)(big falut like we have deployed\nour workflow in AwS and that AWS server itself is down). so ideally our system should be \ncapable enough to handle these 2 fautls.\n\nIf we will create whole workflow in LangChain, LangChain doesn't have Fault tolerance. LIke \nlets say we have create a Chain that has 5 steps and our system is down lets say at 3rd step,\nin that case we need to execute this chain from start. Because LangChain doesn't have any\nconcept of fault tolerance. Fault tolerance means from where our system break from there only\nit will start. LangChains assume it chains are short lived. due to that they think fault tolerance\nis not much important here.\n\nIn LangGraph we have builtin fault tolerance, LangGraph gives fault tolerance in both faults\nsmall or big, for small problems it gives concept of retrying lets say at postjd LinkedIN api\nis not working so we can write our code in try catch if any issue retry. with the help of retry\nlogic we can handle small level faults.\n\nfor big faults, system level faults, our server is down, our docker container is down, in this \nsituation LangGraph has concept of recovery, in this letsay  at 3rd node server is down, in this\nsituation we can resume exaction from here only, and next node will execute it will not happen\nthat from first it will restart again. we get both retry and recovery in built in LangGraph.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "D6LODRt8FySUvUwVbzEca",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1237.1181221627426,
      "y": 107481.90614472446,
      "width": 352,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwI",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1865756394,
      "version": 37,
      "versionNonce": 232772138,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781395198974,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Challenge 5 -> Human in the loop",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Challenge 5 -> Human in the loop",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Ibfb3G_otTBPTK8PckyqI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1299.5282784127426,
      "y": 107515.92958222446,
      "width": 1045,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwJ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 834457514,
      "version": 856,
      "versionNonce": 1163967210,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781395745788,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Like after creating JD LLM need to ask approving JD, or our Agnet is aksing us for postting jd \nand there are multiple scenario,\nUsing LangChain if we want to pause somewhere then in that case that will be stuck their untill\nhuman will not answer, 1 way is we can crete 2 chains one is till asking human second is where\nwe pass whole previous context and  this human concern and our second chain will move forward\nfurther but for this we need to write more glue code.\n\n\nOn the other hand LangGraph provide human in the loop out of the box. This is possible because\nLangGraph checkpoints the graph state after each step, which allows system to persist execution\ncontext and later resume the workflow.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Like after creating JD LLM need to ask approving JD, or our Agnet is aksing us for postting jd \nand there are multiple scenario,\nUsing LangChain if we want to pause somewhere then in that case that will be stuck their untill\nhuman will not answer, 1 way is we can crete 2 chains one is till asking human second is where\nwe pass whole previous context and  this human concern and our second chain will move forward\nfurther but for this we need to write more glue code.\n\n\nOn the other hand LangGraph provide human in the loop out of the box. This is possible because\nLangGraph checkpoints the graph state after each step, which allows system to persist execution\ncontext and later resume the workflow.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5BNugt-lQz38JV2kthpSp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1231.3798409127426,
      "y": 107886.83973847446,
      "width": 341,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 978447146,
      "version": 36,
      "versionNonce": 1913379690,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781395828122,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Challenge 6 -> Nested Workflows",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Challenge 6 -> Nested Workflows",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FpYQs3XO9fzbKejNZeN-q",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1297.3446846627426,
      "y": 107928.32411347446,
      "width": 1045,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwL",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2006637482,
      "version": 1206,
      "versionNonce": 918714614,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781396379806,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "this is not a challenge more of feature, we have talked about workflow, if we want to create\nworkflow inside workflow then we can do that. We can represent any workflow as a graph in Lang\nGraph where each node representing one task. SInce we can create subgraph hence we can create\nany type of nested workflow as well.\n\nConduct interview itself is a big task\n1 we are preparing qus for each candidate\n2 Multiple rounds of interview with evaluation\nsince all the things are done in one conduct interview only, so we can treat this as one nested\nworkflow, and we can connect this nested workflow in our bigger workflow. So we can create\nany no of workflows inside one workflow. And we represent a workflow as a node in our graph.\n\nSo we can create multi agent system with  this nested workflow or using subgraph in LangGraph.\nWith Subgraph we can make our code reusable.  we can create one subgraph for some feature that\nwe can use in multiple place in bigger task.\n\nFor LangChain it is a challenge.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "this is not a challenge more of feature, we have talked about workflow, if we want to create\nworkflow inside workflow then we can do that. We can represent any workflow as a graph in Lang\nGraph where each node representing one task. SInce we can create subgraph hence we can create\nany type of nested workflow as well.\n\nConduct interview itself is a big task\n1 we are preparing qus for each candidate\n2 Multiple rounds of interview with evaluation\nsince all the things are done in one conduct interview only, so we can treat this as one nested\nworkflow, and we can connect this nested workflow in our bigger workflow. So we can create\nany no of workflows inside one workflow. And we represent a workflow as a node in our graph.\n\nSo we can create multi agent system with  this nested workflow or using subgraph in LangGraph.\nWith Subgraph we can make our code reusable.  we can create one subgraph for some feature that\nwe can use in multiple place in bigger task.\n\nFor LangChain it is a challenge.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ZtorK2gZksa6RAqZsq3-c",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1215.3798409127426,
      "y": 108443.83973847446,
      "width": 308,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwM",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 639325366,
      "version": 31,
      "versionNonce": 1990169142,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781396395325,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Challenge 7 -> Observability",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Challenge 7 -> Observability",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "9MNCWmRv5TGj60SwxAW7q",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1275.3017159127426,
      "y": 108477.30848847446,
      "width": 1111,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwN",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2087914602,
      "version": 1668,
      "versionNonce": 1449605366,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781397168580,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "observability refers to how easily we can monitor, debug and understand what your workflow is doing\nat runtime\n\nwe have one library named LangSmith using whihc we can monitor our LLM application in LangChain.\nit will record everything when we have called LLM, what was the prompt, what was the reply, what\nwas the time take, howmany tokens everything LangSmith will record. LangSmith can only monitor\nLangchain but can't monitor our Glue code. It means if we are createing Complex workflow using\nLangchain & Langsmith, we will get only partial observabitlity as it can't observer glue code.\n\n\nWith LangGraph, LangSmith has too tight intergration, in LangGraph whole timeline of events like\nfrom this node to this node everything record in LangSmith. everything Human intervention when\nhuman approval came, when we have aksed for the input, and what is the node before that everything\nLangSmith records. Hence we can backtrack whole workflow using LangSmith.\n\nwhen we deploy our workflow in production, we must monitor very closly. with LangChain we can only\nmonitor partially due to gluecode, but if we are using LangGraph to build our complex workflow, since\nwe are writing everything in LangGraph ,hence LangGraph can tell very well to LangSmith what was\nthe reason for taking this decision at each and every point. it makes monitoring, observability\nvery easy.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "observability refers to how easily we can monitor, debug and understand what your workflow is doing\nat runtime\n\nwe have one library named LangSmith using whihc we can monitor our LLM application in LangChain.\nit will record everything when we have called LLM, what was the prompt, what was the reply, what\nwas the time take, howmany tokens everything LangSmith will record. LangSmith can only monitor\nLangchain but can't monitor our Glue code. It means if we are createing Complex workflow using\nLangchain & Langsmith, we will get only partial observabitlity as it can't observer glue code.\n\n\nWith LangGraph, LangSmith has too tight intergration, in LangGraph whole timeline of events like\nfrom this node to this node everything record in LangSmith. everything Human intervention when\nhuman approval came, when we have aksed for the input, and what is the node before that everything\nLangSmith records. Hence we can backtrack whole workflow using LangSmith.\n\nwhen we deploy our workflow in production, we must monitor very closly. with LangChain we can only\nmonitor partially due to gluecode, but if we are using LangGraph to build our complex workflow, since\nwe are writing everything in LangGraph ,hence LangGraph can tell very well to LangSmith what was\nthe reason for taking this decision at each and every point. it makes monitoring, observability\nvery easy.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
